  "Number": 16,
  "Question": "If \\(F(n)\\) is a function such that \\(F(1)=F(2)=F(3)=1,\\) and such that \\[ F(n+1)=\\frac{F(n)F(n-1)+1}{F(n-2)} \\] for \\(n\\ge3,\\) then \\(F(6)\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\)",
    "C": "\\(7\\)",
    "D": "\\(11\\)",
    "E": "\\(26\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "To find \\(F(6)\\), first calculate the earlier values it needs. Substituting \\(n=3,4,5\\) in order gives\n\\[\nF(4)=\\frac{F(3)F(2)+1}{F(1)}\n=\\frac{1\\cdot1+1}{1}=2,\n\\]\n\\[\nF(5)=\\frac{F(4)F(3)+1}{F(2)}\n=\\frac{2\\cdot1+1}{1}=3,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nF(6)=\\frac{F(5)F(4)+1}{F(3)}\n=\\frac{3\\cdot2+1}{1}=7.\n\\]\nAll denominators used are the given initial values \\(1\\), so each division is valid. Therefore \\(F(6)=7\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
